Just to throw some ideas out... I'm not advocating them, just suggesting
some things to think about.

You could change resistance so it's not binary (I haven't played Spork,
so I don't know).  D&D3e has fire immunity (which is binary), but also
has resistance, which reduces each dose of fire damage by a certain
amount (possibly to 0).  You could therefore remove fire immunity, or
make it harder to get (get that at crowning instead of resistance, or
make it a feature of a =oFireRes, or short-term from a spell, etc.), and
reduce current resistance to a set amount.  In fact, you could base it
on the relative power of the creature granting the intrinsic.  Eat a
dragon, get *lots*.  Eat something much smaller, get a little.  It could
be cumulative (eat *lots* of fire creatures) or not (eat one big one and
be done with it).  You could make fire resistance cumulative another
way, too.  Add up the anti-fire properties of all creatures eaten, and
as you (would have taken) fire damage it gets taken off this instead.
when you run out, you take normal damage again.  So, you might find
yourself protected from a certain amount of fire, but still chose to
avoid things anyway so you don't waste that protection.

If you do that last one, you might have a 'reserve benefit'.  If you
have a certain amount of fire resistance, you might find that the fire
damage you would've taken is reduced a little.  Maybe the amount of fire
damage you're exposed to is reduced by 1/10 of your accumulated fire
resistance, before applying it to you.  If you have 50 points of fire
resistance, and 20 points of fire hits you, you remove only 15 points of
fire resistance.  Perhaps rather than a percentage of the resistance
being applied, maybe the amount of resistance as a percentage is used
(so 50 points of fire resistance would remove 50% of the fire damage
before application; being hit by 20 points of fire would reduce your
resistance by 10).  At 100 points of fire resistance you'd take no
damage from fire.

Perhaps fire resistance or immunity causes vulnerability to cold, or
prevents cold resistance or immunity.  For instance, maybe getting fire
resistance cancels a like amount of cold resistance (and vice-versa);
fire immunity might mean you take extra damage from cold.


Perhaps characters could pick up 'elemental traits' (racially, too).
I'd expect that you could only pursue one set of traits at a tim.

As you become more 'fiery' (eat fire creatures, possibly from taking
fire damage, learn fire-related spells, etc.) you might gain resistance.
You might become better at certain things (get extra benefit from
/oFire, fireball but not magic missile, firebrand, etc.).  You might
suffer some detriments (vulnerability to cold, become more sensitive to
water, have trouble with books, scrolls, and potions, etc.).  You might
find like-traited creatures (fire giants, certain demons, elementals)
more friendly, others (ice giant, other demons, elementals) less so.

Maybe you're stuck with these traits, they could be intrinsics (and
subject to gremlins), maybe they could be reversed, possibly painfully.
Fiery traits might require exposure to cold (with the increased damage
that comes from it), or eating cold-based creatures (which might now be
poisonous to you, regardless of poison immunity, because they are now
counter to your very nature... or cause level loss, which could be a
good-or-bad thing for you).

As mentioned, races could have elemental affinities (start off with some
elemental traits).  Some or all might be prevented from losing all of it
(*all* dwarves might have earth traits, while humans might be able to
shift between them).

Perhaps the degree of trait depends on other matters.  Not just how many
of what kind of creature you've eaten.  The spells you learn, the other
intrinsics you get (I can see invisibility being 'air' -- having earth
traits might prevent you from becoming invisible, that =oInvisibility
might 'do nothing'), possibly the items you wield (especially for a long
time), all of it could affect you.


